>
  <main class="container" style="padding: 34px 0 70px;">
    <article class="card" style="padding: 28px; max-width: 860px;">
      <header style="margin-bottom: 22px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 10px; color: var(--primary); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.02em; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 13px;">
          Maidstone Drylining FAQ
        </p>
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: clamp(26px, 4vw, 36px); line-height: 1.15;">{question}</h1>
      </header>

      <section style="padding: 18px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); margin-bottom: 22px;">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text);">Yes. The cavity behind dot-and-dab plasterboard or within a metal stud partition is the standard place to run electrical cables, switch drops, socket wiring, plumbing pipes, and even radiator feeds. This is one of the big advantages of dry lining over wet plaster.</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text);">Sequence in a typical Maidstone refit:</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text);">1. First-fix electrical: cables run in cavity before boarding
2. First-fix plumbing: pipework run, tested, lagged
3. Plasterboard fixed, leaving access panels where required
4. Second-fix electrical: face plates fitted
5. Jointing and decoration</p>
<p style="margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.8; color: var(--text);">We coordinate with your electrician and plumber (or provide both as part of a full package). All electrical work is carried out to BS 7671 (the IEE Wiring Regulations) by a registered NICEIC or NAPIT electrician.</p>
      </section>

      <section style="margin: 24px 0; padding: 18px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border);">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 12px; color: var(--text);">Related Questions</h3>
        <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0;">
<li><a href="/drylining-faq/what-is-dot-and-dab-dry-lining/" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 500;">What is dot-and-dab dry lining?</a></li>
<li><a href="/drylining-faq/what-is-metal-frame-dry-lining/" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 500;">What is metal frame dry lining?</a></li>
<li><a href="/services/drylining-contractors/" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 500;">Drylining Contractors service</a></li>
        </ul>
